 "duration": 83.41,
 "text": "Four months from today, it is January. What is different if you actually do the thing you keep telling yourself you will do, not the big version of it, the small one? Twenty minutes a day, one page, one phone call. Four months is about 120 days. A hundred and twenty days of almost anything changes something. The work is not the hard part. The hard part is that in three weeks you will not remember you said this. So here is the whole method. It takes five minutes to set up. Write one sentence, not a feeling. A sentence someone else could check, not get fit. Walk to 30 minutes, then pick the smallest version that still counts on a bad day. If your normal is an hour, your bad day is 10 minutes. 10 minutes still counts. Then mark it, every single day. On paper, on a wall, anywhere you will see it without going to look for it. You will miss days. Everyone misses days. The only rule that matters is this. Never miss twice. At 30 days, it stops feeling like a decision. At 60, you stop noticing you are doing it. At 120, you are just someone who does this. That is the whole thing. Write the sentence tonight. In January, you will either have it or you will have the same sentence you have now.",
